{
  "configuration_id": "config_008",
  "reason": "With no prior evidence, the most important uncertainty is whether multimodal fusion (image + RNA) provides meaningful accuracy gains over single-modality baselines, and which component combination yields a strong starting reference. Config_008 (multimodal, multiprofile, scgpt, resnet50, avgpool, weighted) tests this core hypothesis with a well-balanced, moderate-complexity setup: scgpt is a strong pretrained single-cell RNA encoder (more biologically informed than PCA, potentially more expressive than scvi_n200), resnet50 is a solid image backbone that is substantially cheaper than imagenet_vit_l, avgpool is a low-cost aggregation, and weighted fusion is a standard, interpretable fusion strategy. This experiment establishes a strong multimodal baseline at moderate compute cost (estimated ~40-50% of a ViT-L + transformer configuration), leaving ample GPU budget to later explore upgrades (ViT-L, transformer aggregation, scgpt+ViT-L) or single-modality baselines. If it performs well, subsequent experiments can exploit by upgrading individual components; if it underperforms, we gain early signal that multimodal fusion or the chosen encoders need rethinking, saving budget from chasing a weak direction."
}

